`useEffect` runs code after React renders. Use it to synchronize a component with something outside React, such as a browser event, timer, or network connection.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(tick, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(timer);
}, []);
```

The function returned by the effect cleans up the timer. The dependency array controls when the effect runs again: `[]` means after the component mounts; `[value]` means after it mounts and whenever `value` changes.
